<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turfan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Turpan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Turfan, or Turpan, is an oasis city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in the Turfan Depression, one of the lowest and hottest places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Watered by underground karez channels, it was a major stop on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, home to the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> and from the 9th century to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> kingdom of Qocho. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> in many languages found in its ruins are a key source for Central Asian history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turfan.jpg" alt="Turfan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colegota · CC BY-SA 2.5 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turpan-jiaohe-ruinas-d16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Turpan, also written Turfan, is a prefecture-level city in the east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> autonomous region, about 150 kilometres south-east of Ürümqi, on the northern side of the Turpan Depression at about 30 metres above sea level. It has an extremely continental desert climate, with very hot summers, very cold winters and annual precipitation of only 15.7 millimetres. The ancient irrigation system and the summer heat allow the countryside to produce great quantities of high-quality fruit, and the region is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s largest raisin-producing area. The historical centre of the area has shifted, from Jiaohe to Qocho, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>, and to Turpan itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Jushi Kingdom ruled the area in the 1st millennium BCE until the Chinese Han dynasty conquered it in 107 BCE. Han and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> contended for it. After the fall of the Han in 220 the region was virtually independent. Tocharian-speaking people lived in Turpan and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> up to at least the 8th century, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> settled there. In 327 the Former Liang created the Gaochang Commandery here, and from 460 Gaochang was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a> under a Han Chinese king. Later kings of the Qu dynasty were vassals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> conquered the region in 640 and renamed it Xi Prefecture; Sogdian and Chinese merchants traded extensively, and contracts found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana-cemetery">Astana Cemetery</a> record sales of slaves. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> took Turfan in 792, and in 803 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> seized it from them. After the Kirghiz destroyed the Uyghur Khaganate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> in 840, many Uyghurs moved here. The Uyghurs set up the Idikut kingdom, also called the Kara-Khoja Kingdom, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>, which lasted from 856 to 1389. Its people were first Manichaean and later converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, funding the Bezeklik Caves, and the kingdom became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Kara-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>German expeditions between 1902 and 1914 took paintings and other art to the Museum of Asian Art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and many of the artefacts of Manichaean and Buddhist origin were later destroyed or looted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The Turfan fragments, over 40,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and fragments in 16 languages, are kept in the Berlin State Library. They hold Buddhist, Christian-Nestorian, Manichaean and secular texts, among them parts of the Gospel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a>. Many texts survive because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a> graveyard used discarded paper to make clothing for corpses. The conversion of the Buddhist population to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was completed in the second half of the 15th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Turpan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late fifteenth century it split between rival khans and gave way to a separate eastern khanate based at <b>Turpan</b> and to the Yarkent Khanate founded in 1514.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the east they took the western Tarim Basin from the weakening Rouran, <b>Turfan</b> in 479 and the Urumchi region in 497–509.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Subject groups such as the Gaoche and the Turks of the Altai, who worked iron for them, supplied livestock, warriors and metal, and the oasis states of <b>Turfan</b> and the Tarim Basin paid tribute and at times accepted Rouran-backed rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Uyghurs who fled south founded the Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom in modern Gansu and the Kingdom of Qocho near <b>Turpan</b>, where they turned to Buddhism and, in Mahmud al-Kashgari's words, were 'the strongest of the infidels'; Kashgari praised their Turkic as 'pure' and 'most correct'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>If the Yuezhi spoke a Tocharian language, as many scholars believe, they are also connected with the Tocharian Buddhist texts of Kucha and <b>Turfan</b>, written between about the sixth and eighth centuries CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>German expeditions to <b>Turfan</b> in Xinjiang, from 1902, recovered thousands of fragments of Manichaean texts in Middle Persian, Parthian, Sogdian and Old Uyghur, together with illuminated pages of exquisite book painting.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
